From f960fe191c662475dd83b72832e70afc44778877 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 00:46:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] VideoRenderers: smooth enlarged image subtitles with Scale2x

Image subtitles are authored far below the screen resolution - PGS on a
UHD disc is a 1920x1080 canvas, a DVD SPU 720x480 - and are stretched to
the display with GL_LINEAR, so on a 4K screen every source pixel becomes
a block and the lettering shows its own pixel grid as a staircase along
each curve.

No resampling kernel removes that. They all reconstruct from the same
quantised samples, so a sharper one draws the staircase more crisply
rather than removing it, and the sharper ones ring: measured on a DVD
subtitle, Catmull-Rom puts 9.5% of pixels outside 0..1 and Lanczos3
12.2%, reaching -0.33 and +1.36, which clips to halos on these edges.

Scale2x (EPX) works on the geometry instead. Each pixel becomes four, and
a corner takes a neighbour's colour only where the two neighbours meeting
at it match each other and the opposite pair does not - the signature of
a staircase step, which does not occur inside a flat area or along a
straight edge. It only copies colours already present, so it cannot
invent a tone, cannot ring, and cannot change a shape beyond rounding a
step corner.

Measured against the same shape drawn directly at the output size, mean
absolute error per pixel: bilinear 4.52, Mitchell 4.49, Lanczos3 4.49,
Catmull-Rom 4.06, Scale2x twice 2.39. On a shape of the diagonals and
arcs letters are made of, bilinear 5.19 against Scale2x 2.39.

Only whole doublings that still fit inside the area the subtitle is drawn
into are taken, so a 1080p canvas on a 4K screen is doubled once and the
same canvas at 1080p is left alone. A larger texture than its destination
would only be averaged back down by the driver. There is nothing to
configure, and where no doubling fits the path is exactly as before.

The doubling runs once per subtitle image, before upload, on the thread
that draws it, so it is also held to a texture budget: an ordinary line of
subtitle text costs a megabyte or two, but a full-frame graphic doubled
twice would run to tens of them. The drawn geometry is untouched - only
the texture is larger - so position, size and alignment are unchanged.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
